---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const repo = "https://github.com/shubham-bansal-jswone/plates-and-bars/blob/main";
---

<Base title="Licences - Plate & Bar" description="Open-source and content licences used by Plate & Bar.">
  <div class="doc"><div class="prose">
  <h1>Licences</h1>

  <h2>Our code</h2>
  <p>The source code of Plate &amp; Bar is licensed under the Apache License 2.0. See <a href={`${repo}/LICENSE`}>LICENSE</a> and <a href={`${repo}/NOTICE`}>NOTICE</a>.</p>

  <h2>Our content</h2>
  <p>Content we wrote ourselves (exercise descriptions, cards, recipes and similar) is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>). You may share and adapt it if you credit Plate &amp; Bar and say if you made changes. See <a href={`${repo}/CONTENT-LICENSE`}>CONTENT-LICENSE</a>.</p>

  <h2>Open Food Facts</h2>
  <p>When you scan a barcode, our server looks the product up on <a href="https://world.openfoodfacts.org">Open Food Facts</a>. The Open Food Facts database is made available under the <a href="https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/1-0/">Open Database Licence (ODbL) 1.0</a> and its individual contents under the <a href="https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/dbcl/1-0/">Database Contents Licence (DbCL) 1.0</a>. The product facts we keep in our cache stay under ODbL and DbCL, with credit to Open Food Facts and its contributors. We fetch text and number fields only, not product images (those are CC BY-SA 3.0). The data is entered by the community and may contain errors; check it against the pack. It is not medical advice.</p>

  <h2>Fonts and icons</h2>
  <p>The site and app use Roboto and Inter, both under the SIL Open Font License 1.1, and the app (not the site) uses the Ionicons icon font (MIT).</p>

  <h2>Third-party software</h2>
  <p>The app bundles open-source packages under their own licences, mostly MIT, BSD, ISC and Apache 2.0. <a href={`${repo}/THIRD_PARTY.md`}>THIRD_PARTY.md</a> lists our data sources, fonts and icons, and the npm and backend dependencies under any other licence. The Android app's About screen, under Open-source licences, lists the app's production npm packages, every Java/Kotlin library on the release build's classpath and the third-party native code linked into the app, with its licence text and any NOTICE file it ships.</p>
  </div></div>
</Base>
